{
  "_disclaimer": "SIMULATED DEMONSTRATION THRESHOLDS -- NOT CLINICAL GUIDELINES. These values were written by the project team to be internally plausible for a prototype. They are not drawn from any validated source, have not been reviewed by a clinician, and must never be used for real triage.",
  "_reading_the_bands": "For each vital: critical_low < low < high < critical_high. A value outside low..high is a MILD deviation. A value outside critical_low..critical_high is a SEVERE deviation. A null bound means that direction is not scored. spo2 has no upper bound.",
  "_why_age_matters": "A heart rate of 168 is an emergency in a 58-year-old and unremarkable in an eight-month-old. A temperature of 37.9 is nothing in an adult and is worth noticing in a frail 80-year-old who may not mount a fever at all. One adult-calibrated table is not a simplification, it is a safety defect. These five tables exist so the same number can mean different things.",
  "_geriatric_note": "The geriatric table is narrower than the adult table in both directions. Older patients decompensate with smaller deviations, are more vulnerable to hypothermia, and often mount a blunted fever response, so the upper temperature bound is LOWER than the adult one rather than higher.",

